      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by finding her walking rate in miles per minute. We know that Jana takes 24 minutes to walk one mile. Therefore, her walking rate is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Rate} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. We do this by multiplying her walking rate by the number of minutes we are interested in (10 minutes):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\text{Rate} \\times \\text{Time} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expression, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo express this as a decimal to the nearest tenth, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} \\approx 0.416667\n\\]\n\nRounding 0.416667 to the nearest tenth gives us:\n\n\\[\n0.4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
