      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine how far Jana will walk in 10 minutes, we start by noting her walking rate. Jana's walking rate is 1 mile per 24 minutes. This can be expressed as a fraction of miles per minute:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Walking rate} = \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find out how far she will walk in 10 minutes. To do this, we multiply her walking rate by the number of minutes she will be walking:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\left( \\frac{1 \\text{ mile}}{24 \\text{ minutes}} \\right) \\times 10 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side of the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\frac{10}{24} \\text{ miles}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{10}{24}\\), we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 10 and 24, which is 2. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{10}{24} = \\frac{10 \\div 2}{24 \\div 2} = \\frac{5}{12}\n\\]\n\nNext, we convert the fraction \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) to a decimal. We do this by performing the division:\n\n\\[\n5 \\div 12 \\approx 0.4167\n\\]\n\nRounding 0.4167 to the nearest tenth, we get:\n\n\\[\n0.4167 \\approx 0.4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance Jana will walk in 10 minutes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.4}\n\\]",
